import DocsLayout from "../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";

const compileCode = `let definition = ProviderNativeAgentGraph::compile(
    typed_definition.descriptor().clone(),
    graph_identity,
    reducer_identity,
    state_schema_id,
    input_security_label,
    policy,
    accounting,
)?;

definition.register_schema(&mut schemas)?;
register_standard_agent_cancellation_event_schema(&mut schemas)?;`;
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Run the provider-native model/tool graph."
  description="Compile one digest-pinned Agent composition, execute sequential model/tool turns from durable evidence, and confirm cancellation without inventing usage."
  section="Provider-native Agent"
>
  <aside
    class="docs-callout docs-callout--boundary"
    aria-labelledby="provider-native-scope-title"
  >
    <h2 id="provider-native-scope-title">Implemented pre-alpha boundary</h2>
    <p>
      The prebuilt graph, local policy boundary, exact invocation accounting,
      multi-turn transcript assembly, durable cancellation observation, and
      lifecycle confirmation are implemented. The crate remains unpublished;
      parallel tools and output repair have not shipped. <a
        href="/docs/agent-service/">AgentService v1</a
      > now exposes this definition through an embedding boundary, and the <a
        href="/docs/mcp-remote-tool/">strict MCP Remote Tool profile</a
      > plus <a href="/docs/a2a-client/">A2A Remote Agent profile</a> supply client-side
      Tool adapters. Stable network transport, broader protocol extensions, and production
      qualification remain ahead.
    </p>
  </aside>

  <section aria-labelledby="provider-native-example-title">
    <h2 id="provider-native-example-title">Compile the deployment contract</h2>
    <p>
      Start with the checked-in example. It builds a real tool-capable Agent,
      strict read-only policy, explicit unpriced accounting, generated state
      schema, standard runtime schemas, and initial checkpoint state. It needs
      no API key or database and performs no external I/O.
    </p>
    <div class="docs-code" data-code-block>
      <div class="docs-code-head">
        <span>Shell · deterministic and offline</span>
        <button
          class="copy-button affordance"
          type="button"
          data-copy-button
          data-copy-target="provider-native-example-command"
        >
          <span data-copy-label aria-live="polite">Copy</span>
        </button>
      </div>
      <pre
        id="provider-native-example-command"
        tabindex="0"><code>cargo run -p stateknot-runtime --example provider_native_agent --locked</code></pre>
    </div>
    <p>
      Read the exact <a
        href="https://github.com/StateKnot/StateKnot/blob/main/crates/stateknot-runtime/examples/provider_native_agent.rs"
        >compiled example source</a
      > before adapting it. The example stops at the deployment boundary; the PostgreSQL
      suite proves dispatch and recovery separately.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="provider-native-freeze-title">
    <h2 id="provider-native-freeze-title">
      Pin code, policy, and accounting together
    </h2>
    <p>
      The composition digest binds the Agent, Model, Tool, instructions,
      execution limits, input security label, policy implementation, and
      accounting tables. Changing any one of them requires a new graph version.
    </p>
    <div class="docs-code" data-code-block>
      <div class="docs-code-head">
        <span>Rust · startup composition</span>
        <button
          class="copy-button affordance"
          type="button"
          data-copy-button
          data-copy-target="provider-native-compile-code"
        >
          <span data-copy-label aria-live="polite">Copy</span>
        </button>
      </div>
      <pre
        id="provider-native-compile-code"
        tabindex="0"><code>{compileCode}</code></pre>
    </div>
    <p>
      Register every typed input/output schema, Tool schema, provider profile,
      and standard runtime event schema before freezing the registry. Startup
      fails on a missing or conflicting digest. Existing runs always reload the
      graph version pinned by their checkpoint.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="provider-native-turn-title">
    <h2 id="provider-native-turn-title">Follow one durable turn</h2>
    <ol class="evidence-list">
      <li>
        <h3>Reconstruct</h3>
        <p>
          The model node rebuilds provider-native transcript order and
          cumulative usage from immutable invocation ledgers.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Commit before dispatch</h3>
        <p>
          The logical invocation and physical attempt start become durable
          before provider or Tool I/O. An idempotently observed start never
          grants dispatch authority.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Authorize each action</h3>
        <p>
          Canonical Tool arguments, proposal order, admission, and model
          invocation are bound into an action digest. Policy evidence is pinned
          before Tool preparation.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Advance from ledgers</h3>
        <p>
          Exact terminal revisions become the node result. A crash reloads
          committed evidence instead of redispatching the external call.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      Known Tool failures remain ordered transcript outcomes. An ambiguous write
      stays <code>Unknown</code> until a status query, idempotency proof, compensation,
      or human decision reconciles it.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="provider-native-cancel-title">
    <h2 id="provider-native-cancel-title">
      Treat cancellation as two durable facts
    </h2>
    <dl class="docs-definition-list">
      <div>
        <dt>Request</dt>
        <dd>
          An authenticated control plane appends immutable cancellation intent.
          The Driver polls durable state, stops new dispatch, signals
          cooperative cancellation, and applies a bounded grace period.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Confirm</dt>
        <dd>
          The Agent Loop carries the exact checkpoint, journal head, revision,
          failure ID, and lease to lifecycle. Lifecycle reconstructs usage and
          atomically confirms cancellation with database time.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Fail closed</dt>
        <dd>
          Executing models, Unknown writes, or missing model usage keep the run
          <code>cancellation_requested</code>. StateKnot never substitutes zero
          usage for unavailable evidence.
        </dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
    <p>
      The public-safe confirmation schema is available at <a
        href="/schemas/runtime/agent-cancellation-event/1.0.0"
        >its stable runtime identity</a
      >. <a href="/docs/agent-service/">AgentService v1</a> now supplies the authorization-first
      cancellation ingress; an HTTP/gRPC transport remains an embedding-service responsibility
      in this pre-alpha cut.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="provider-native-proof-title">
    <h2 id="provider-native-proof-title">Run the PostgreSQL proof</h2>
    <p>
      After configuring the test database as described in the <a
        href="/docs/postgresql/">PostgreSQL guide</a
      >, require the integration suite instead of allowing an infrastructure
      skip.
    </p>
    <div class="docs-code" data-code-block>
      <div class="docs-code-head">
        <span>Shell · PostgreSQL 16 or 17</span>
        <button
          class="copy-button affordance"
          type="button"
          data-copy-button
          data-copy-target="provider-native-test-command"
        >
          <span data-copy-label aria-live="polite">Copy</span>
        </button>
      </div>
      <pre
        id="provider-native-test-command"
        tabindex="0"><code>STATEKNOT_REQUIRE_POSTGRES_TESTS=1 \
cargo test -p stateknot-runtime --test postgres provider_native --locked</code></pre>
    </div>
    <p>
      The focused cases cover multi-turn recovery without model redispatch,
      stale-fence policy races, known failed Tools, exact-usage cancellation,
      pre-dispatch cancellation, and evidence-unavailable fail-closed behavior
      on both supported PostgreSQL majors.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="provider-native-boundary-title">
    <h2 id="provider-native-boundary-title">
      Keep the release boundary visible
    </h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Sequential Tool execution is implemented; parallel Tool and Graph
        sibling ordering is not.
      </li>
      <li>
        Model-native structured output is implemented; output repair and
        Tool-call-emulated final output are rejected.
      </li>
      <li>
        Exact cancellation confirmation and the AgentService v1 embedding
        ingress are implemented; stable HTTP/gRPC transport is not.
      </li>
      <li>
        The provider-native execution core, strict MCP Remote Tool, and A2A
        Remote Agent bindings are implemented. Independent MCP/A2A Server
        profiles exist but are not composed into this Graph. Broader protocol
        extensions, A2A live-peer qualification/reconciliation, artifact
        retrieval, failover, and a production release remain ahead.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The complete integration and operations contract lives in the <a
        href="https://github.com/StateKnot/StateKnot/blob/main/docs/provider-native-agent.md"
        >repository guide</a
      >.
    </p>
  </section>
</DocsLayout>
